    "body": "On 5/13/2020 5:25 PM, Domenic Denicola wrote:\n> What part of the description led you to think that it could be a text string?\n> \n> In general, we don't give a ton of detail in these \"intro\" boxes; that's best left to places like MDN. We just want to summarize the basics of the API. This is especially true for legacy APIs like the table accessors in that section.\n> \n\nThanks for your quick response.\n\n\nSimply writing\n\n  table . caption [ = value ]\n\n\n\nseems to imply, to me anyway, that 'value' is\njust a string. I had to run a series of\nexperiments to actually get a value into\na caption (I'm writing some courses on\nweb development and I'm not at a place where\nI can have the students use innerHTML like\nW3Schools does - I have to use more basic\ncoding).\n\n\nSo I'm ending up writing like this:\n\n\n* return the caption _element_ (note: this is\n   _not_ the text in the caption):\n\n\t\tmyCap = tnode.caption;\n\n\n* return the length of the text in a caption element:\n\n\t\tcLen = myCap.firstChild.length;\n\n\n* return the text in the caption element:\n\n\t\tcapText = myCap.firstChild.substringData(0,cLen);\n\n\n* set the value of the caption:\n\n\t\tmyCap.firstChild.replaceData(0,cLen,alt_text);\n\n\nI'm glad I did this, however, otherwise I would have misled\nmy students by using the syntax shown in the document.\n\n\nKind regards,\n\n\n-Steve Comstock\n\n",
