    "body": "On 5/14/2020 5:59 AM, Anne van Kesteren wrote:\n> We're entering Stack Overflow territory, \n\nHmmm. I don't know what that implies; all unchartered\nterritory for me right now.\n\nbut roughly, elements in HTML map to classes, which are called \ninterfaces in IDL. Confusingly, both elements and interfaces have things \ncalled attributes. While they often seem similar, e.g., `lang=\"\"` and \n`.lang`, they are distinct and defined independently. `textContent` is \nan attribute/property that only exists on an interface. I.e., you cannot \nwrite `<html textContent=\"...\">` and therefore it is not a global \nattribute (those are attributes for elements). Hope that helps.\n> \n\nAh, but I find, then, that these statements both work:\n\n\n<p onclick=\"alert(document.getElementById('tTable').caption.textContent);\">\nClick here to view caption\n</p>\n\n\n<p onclick=\"document.getElementById('tTable').caption.textContent='New \nCaption';\">Click here to reset caption\n</p>\n\n\nwhich raises questions for me:\n\n* Is 'caption' a _property_ of table elements?\n    (I know it's not an attribute, since you don't\n     code it inside the start tag)\n\n    but the code seems to behave as if this is a property\n\n\n   [I notice that in the HTML standard, the write up\n    for table elements, in the IDL Interface section,\n    'caption' is listed as an attribute, in the sense\n    that Anne pointed out]\n\n\n* Is 'textContent' a property of caption elements?\n    (I see from the DOM standard that 'textContent'\n     has a wider useage also)\n\n    [I notice in the HTML standard, the write up\n     for caption elements does not list 'textContent'\n     anywhere]\n\n\n    and yet the code works.\n\n\nI understand authors of the standard are trying to be\nprecise, and also that there are historical reasons for\nsome anomalies and exceptions that are out there. So I\nappreciate all the work that continously goes in to these\nefforts. Just trying to get some clarifications; and\nmaybe these might lead to slight changes in the standard\n(or ... maybe not. :-) )\n\n\nIn any case, I'm glad to have learned some new techniques\nthat I can put into my course.\n\n\nKind regards,\n\n\n-Steve\n\n\n",
